n  and that's that's what's so important  about this case  there were three witnesses who said that  your client mr green confessed to the  murder  yes yes that is unfortunately true and  all three of them have recanted that  testimony  and  that's important and the courts  often look uh with disdain on recanted  testimony  but this case is a little bit different  because uh the brevard county  prosecutor's office before they  prosecuted mr greene prosecuted three  other people  with similar jailhouse confessions  and those three other people were later  exonerated  those jail house confessions were  demonstrated to be just as false as the  jailhouse confessions that um you know  contributed to mr greene's  conviction  among those stepping forward to support  crosley greene is one of the police  officers from the original investigation  diane clark  when i put a badge on i took an oath to  serve and protect the community and that  meant all community in this particular  case with crosley green the truth was  hidden  and to me that's a travesty of justice  he spent half his life in prison for  something  that i don't believe he did  and i hope the right the wrong can be  righted you're not angry at the  prosecutors who uh withheld evidence  that might have exonerated you  no  no  i'm not hanging  i'm not much angry at kim hill hilla  i'm not much angry at  stuff that has happened  that i'm not gonna speak about doing the  case and stuff like that but  i looked back at it and there was reason  some things had happened  you know  for protection-wise or something like  that  you know so that went against me but i'm  not angry  let me ask you how do you account for  your client's continued hope and  optimism in the face of  what appears to be such a miscarriage of  justice  my client is one of the most amazing  people i've ever met  um things he has faced that would  destroy most humans  um  he just has overcome  based entirely on his faith in god and  it's a beautiful t